<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Periodic `simpool reap`. Copy to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ and load it:

      cp contrib/com.simpool.reap.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.simpool.reap.plist

  Why this exists: nothing runs `simpool reap` on its own. `with` deliberately
  does not shut simulators down on exit (see "Architecture" in the README), so
  without a schedule the only cleanup that ever happens is whatever a human
  types. On the machine this was written for, that meant 452 processes from 19
  deleted simulators surviving 18 days, 15.3 GB of swap in use, and the Mac
  paging itself to a standstill while `simctl` looked healthy.

  The flags below are one policy, not the only one:

  --max is ON BY DEFAULT and is deliberately NOT passed here: bring any group
  holding more slots than its cap back down to it, deleting the excess
  simulators. Left off the command line, the flag resolves the same way every
  other simpool caller resolves it — SIMPOOL_MAX_SLOTS if set, otherwise 3 —
  so this job agrees with your acquirers by construction. Passing "--max 3"
  explicitly would look harmless and silently win over SIMPOOL_MAX_SLOTS, which
  is exactly the reaper-smaller-than-acquirers mismatch that has this job
  deleting simulators the acquirers immediately recreate. If you do need a
  different cap here, set it through EnvironmentVariables below rather than as
  a flag:

      <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
      <dict><key>SIMPOOL_MAX_SLOTS</key><string>5</string></dict>

  A 30-minute idle grace keeps a mismatch loud and slow rather than silent and
  immediate, but it does not make one harmless.

    --purge-orphan-runtimes  kill the userland of devices that no longer
                             exist — only ever processes whose device is
                             absent from a device listing that succeeded and
                             returned other devices.
    --cold 60                shut down free simulators idle for over an hour.
                             Drop this if you would rather keep every slot
                             warm and pay the memory.
    --warm 2                 keep two booted per device+OS group, so the next
                             acquisition does not pay a cold boot.

  Deliberately NOT here: --purge (deletes simulators outright by idle time)
  and --purge-orphans (deletes orphaned devices). Both are destructive to
  devices rather than to dead processes, and neither should run unattended.
  --max also deletes devices, but only ones the pool's own declared cap says
  should never have existed, which is a different kind of decision.

  Adjust the simpool path if you did not install via Homebrew: launchd jobs
  inherit PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, so it must be absolute.
-->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.simpool.reap</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key><array>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/simpool</string>
    <string>reap</string>
    <string>--purge-orphan-runtimes</string>
    <string>--cold</string><string>60</string>
    <string>--warm</string><string>2</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>1800</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/simpool-reap.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/simpool-reap.log</string>
</dict></plist>
